Legally speaking, the word "NetHack" is nowhere near long
enough nor distinctive enough to be eligible for copyright
protection.  Even if you say that it's not just one word but
two words mashed together, you can't copyright two words.
(If you could, NetHack itself would be in some rather serious
legal trouble over some of the hallucinated monster names.)

It could be trademarked, I suppose, but wouldn't there
be some indication if that were the case?

The NetHack source files (and probably any official binaries
as well) are of course covered by copyright, but they're also
covered by the license.

IANAL, but I don't see a legal problem.

There's a politeness issue, but it could be argued that we're past
the statute of limitations on that, as the last release came out
during the Debian Woody epoch.  To put that in perspective, Woody
and the next THREE releases (Sarge and Etch and Lenny) are all so
old they're no longer receiving even critical security updates,
and the repositories for them can no longer be found at the
original addresses (you have to specify archive.debian.org now).
That's Debian, the slowest of the slow open-source distributions.
Of the leading[1] web browsers now, MSIE is the only one that had
released version 1.0 or higher by then.  Emacs, which is infamous
for a cathedral model and painfully slow development, has had two
major and numerous minor releases in that time, going from version
21.1 to version 23.4, and the devs discuss ongoing development in
public from time to time between releases (just not quite as much
as some people would like).  Microsoft, in that time, has released
two service packs for Windows XP, then Windows Vista, two service
packs for Vista, then Windows Seven, a service pack for Seven,
and first a Developer Preview then more recently a Consumer
Preview for the upcoming version Eight.

So it's not like people are being ridiculously impatient here.

[1] I mean the ones that between them have more than 90%
    of the market share.  If you look at less widely deployed
    browsers, Safari is just barely older than NetHack 3.4.3
    (but open-source Webkit is not), and Opera is actually
    older than IE and has managed to retain a stable market
    share of between 0.5% and 2% the entire time, which is
    kind of impressive, especially considering that its
    licensing/finance model has undergone major changes twice.


